WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.678
Good morning, and welcome to today's
presentation titled, Smart Glass,

00:00:14.678 --> 00:00:19.010
How Metal Ions Might Revolutionize Tinting
and Energy Efficiency.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.545
My name is Joe Garcia, and I'm the Program
Manager for the Energy Innovation and

00:00:23.545 --> 00:00:25.492
Emerging Technologies Program.

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:27.703
Today's feature presenter is Mike McGehee,

00:00:27.703 --> 00:00:31.670
Mike McGehee is a professor in a Material
Science and Engineering Department, and

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:35.070
a senior fellow of
the Precourt Institute for Energy.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.919
His research interest are developing
new materials for smart windows and

00:00:38.919 --> 00:00:39.671
solar cells.

00:00:39.671 --> 00:00:43.833
He has taught courses on nanotechnology,
nanocharacterization,

00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:48.330
organic semi-conductors,
polymer science and solar cells.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:52.139
He received his undergraduate degree in
Physics from Princeton University, and

00:00:52.139 --> 00:00:55.907
his PhD in Material Science from the
University of California at Santa Barbara.

00:00:55.907 --> 00:01:00.360
Where he did research on polymer lasers
in a lab of Nobel Laureate Alan Heeger.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.066
Now I'd like to turn
the floor over to Mike.

00:01:04.845 --> 00:01:07.854
&gt;&gt; Thank you for
participating in this webinar.

00:01:10.054 --> 00:01:14.739
Now, today I'm going to tell you
about an exciting dynamic window

00:01:14.739 --> 00:01:19.560
technology that the students and
post docs in my lab have developed and

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.320
on this very first slide,
you can go ahead and see it.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.487
On the left,
the window is in it's clear state, and

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:33.361
then in the next picture to the right,
you can see it partially tinting and

00:01:33.361 --> 00:01:36.418
you can see a nice neutral gray color.

00:01:36.418 --> 00:01:41.843
And then, if you go over one more you
can see it in a nice black opaque state,

00:01:41.843 --> 00:01:44.855
and then if you go all
the way to the right,

00:01:44.855 --> 00:01:49.797
you see that the window has switched
back to the transparent state.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:58.372
So let me step back and motivate on
why we want to have these windows.

00:01:58.372 --> 00:02:02.198
When I look forward into
the future it's hard for

00:02:02.198 --> 00:02:07.049
me to believe that we're going
to have self driving cars, and

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:11.530
yet still be controlling glare
by rotating metal flats,

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:15.990
like you can see with
the window blinds on the left.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:20.500
And that picture illustrates
how sometimes the blinds

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.937
can get all hung up and
not work very well.

00:02:23.937 --> 00:02:28.860
On the right, you see an example where
half of the window is letting too much

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:35.220
light in and there's a lot of glare, and
then on the other half, there is tinting.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.860
And it illustrates that really
the best way to control glare

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:44.740
is not to completely block the light
with blinds or curtains, but

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:50.090
rather to just attenuate it more
like when you're wearing sunglasses,

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:53.750
that way you can still enjoy the view.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:59.895
And we're not just developing
dynamic windows because it'll

00:02:59.895 --> 00:03:05.405
create a more pleasing environment
inside all kinds of buildings and

00:03:05.405 --> 00:03:09.779
cars and airplanes,
it's also about saving energy.

00:03:09.779 --> 00:03:15.191
And this slide has some data that
was collected by new dynamic glass,

00:03:15.191 --> 00:03:20.697
and they show that the energy savings
that you can have with cooling and

00:03:20.697 --> 00:03:23.190
heating and lighting.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:28.935
If you have dynamic windows and
they're intelligently controlled,

00:03:28.935 --> 00:03:34.192
such that they're letting in
light under certain conditions so

00:03:34.192 --> 00:03:40.150
that people can use the natural light and
use less artificial lighting.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:44.829
And in the winter the light is
coming in to heat the building, but

00:03:44.829 --> 00:03:50.464
during other conditions, in the summer
when that unwanted heat is resulting

00:03:50.464 --> 00:03:55.710
in the need for air conditioning,
then the windows can block it.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:04:02.238
And the studies show that
approximately 20% of the heating and

00:04:02.238 --> 00:04:06.986
cooling and ventilation and
lighting costs can

00:04:06.986 --> 00:04:11.743
be saved if dynamic
windows are properly used.

00:04:13.558 --> 00:04:18.506
And then in this is next slide
you can see something from the,

00:04:18.506 --> 00:04:25.710
from view glasses website and their window
is switching between clear and dark.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:29.826
And I won't show their video right now,
but

00:04:29.826 --> 00:04:34.500
there's a web address for
it, and you can hear one

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.633
company explain how their
control system would work.

00:04:39.633 --> 00:04:45.177
And it would have weather data It would
have data from light sensors on top of

00:04:45.177 --> 00:04:52.170
the building, it would know the layout of
the building and where people's desks are.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:56.948
And it would make good decisions
on what the tinting of

00:04:56.948 --> 00:05:02.270
the various windows should be
at various points in the day.

00:05:05.188 --> 00:05:09.845
Now I want to talk about
some of the approaches that

00:05:09.845 --> 00:05:14.120
people are using to control tinting.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:20.665
A lot of people ask me how the windows
that I'm going to tell you about later

00:05:20.665 --> 00:05:25.697
are different from eyeglasses
with dynamic tinting.

00:05:25.697 --> 00:05:29.209
Those have photochromic
materials in them and

00:05:29.209 --> 00:05:33.430
they darken when they're
exposed to UV light.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:36.480
And one thing that's
really nice about them,

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.860
it's all automatic and
there's no need for a power supply.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:46.809
However, some of the downsides,
they don't darken in

00:05:46.809 --> 00:05:51.601
a car because the car windows
filter all of the UV light.

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:56.531
So, you still need sunglasses for
driving in a car.

00:05:56.531 --> 00:06:00.458
And if you don't like
the tinting state that arises,

00:06:00.458 --> 00:06:03.780
there's really nothing
you can do about it.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:09.910
If you're outside and
you're in a photograph for example and you

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:15.590
don't want the eyeglasses to go dark,
you can override it.

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:20.615
And also let's say you're skiing and
it's extremely bright.

00:06:20.615 --> 00:06:24.474
Photochromics don't have a wide range,

00:06:24.474 --> 00:06:28.781
they can't go down to say 1% transmission.

00:06:28.781 --> 00:06:33.747
So it would be good to have a technology
with more control and a wider range,

00:06:35.965 --> 00:06:39.740
Another approach are suspended
particle devices.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:44.993
And here there are particles sandwiched
between a couple of electrodes.

00:06:44.993 --> 00:06:48.549
And applying a field can
line the particles up and

00:06:48.549 --> 00:06:52.270
then light can go in
between the particles.

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:57.830
When there's no field the particles
take more of a random distribution and

00:06:57.830 --> 00:06:59.420
block more of the light.

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:04.306
And this is already being used
in the sky roof of some cars.

00:07:04.306 --> 00:07:09.076
But I've heard various people
say that it's too expensive and

00:07:09.076 --> 00:07:11.597
others say that it's very hazy.

00:07:14.167 --> 00:07:17.707
So that brings me to electrochromics,

00:07:17.707 --> 00:07:23.470
which is probably the leader for
window applications.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:28.006
And in an electrochromic device there
are two transparent electrodes,

00:07:28.006 --> 00:07:30.171
usually made of indium tin oxide.

00:07:30.171 --> 00:07:33.882
And on at least one of them,
there's an electrochromic material,

00:07:33.882 --> 00:07:35.960
such as tungsten oxide.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:40.780
And if voltage is applied
in one direction,

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:46.040
then the tungsten oxide is reduced,
lithium comes into it.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:54.529
And in that state, the material is
much darker although it's not gray or

00:07:54.529 --> 00:07:58.995
black, it tends to be somewhat blue.

00:07:58.995 --> 00:08:02.930
And then on the other electrode,
there're some counter material

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:06.370
Sometimes it's electrochromic and
it switches color too.

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:11.405
Sometimes it's just a transparent
material that helps allow

00:08:11.405 --> 00:08:16.353
the electrochemical reactions
to take place on both sides.

00:08:18.211 --> 00:08:21.785
Tungsten oxide is typically sputtered.

00:08:21.785 --> 00:08:27.600
And in these devices,
both of the transparent electrodes,

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:31.490
the electrochromic, the electrolyte and
the counter material,

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:37.020
they are all sputtered so
there can be a couple microns of material.

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:41.860
And that means that you need a long

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:47.530
line of sputter tools or have a lower
throughput on a smaller system.

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:49.500
So it is somewhat expensive.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:55.290
Another problem is that
sometimes there are pinholes,

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:58.360
in other words,
regions missing the material.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:01.672
And so those spots don't switch color.

00:09:01.672 --> 00:09:04.900
And so typically the yield
is only about one-third,

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:10.510
and that means that two-thirds of
the windows are having to be thrown out.

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:14.040
And I think those are the primary reasons

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:19.680
why this technology is at
about $50 per square foot.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.933
And I've heard from people
in the industry that $20 to

00:09:24.933 --> 00:09:29.970
$30 per square foot is probably where

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:34.480
the technology would be much more
widely adopted than it currently is.

00:09:36.615 --> 00:09:40.255
There is a lot of excitement for this
technology and I think there's a lot of

00:09:40.255 --> 00:09:45.875
sense that something big is about
to happen right around the corner.

00:09:45.875 --> 00:09:50.321
And companies like Kinestral and
View and Sage,

00:09:50.321 --> 00:09:55.749
they don't usually reveal
exactly what they're making.

00:09:55.749 --> 00:10:01.230
But it's probably tungsten oxide or
some similar metal oxide.

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:06.080
And you can see Kinestral
has raised 100 million,

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:10.440
View has raised at least 650 million, and

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:15.430
SageGlass was acquired by Saint-Gobain.

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:19.490
And then there are other
smaller companies as well that

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:24.540
are working on innovative
dynamic glass technologies.

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:31.910
Probably the most commercially successful
electrochromic company is Gentex.

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:35.473
And they use a molecule, methyl viologen,

00:10:35.473 --> 00:10:40.011
that switches colors when
it's oxidized and reduced.

00:10:40.011 --> 00:10:48.120
And they chose an application
that's an easier one than Windows.

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:52.850
They make the rear view mirrors for
cars that have a light sensor.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:59.750
And if someone is driving behind the car
and the high beams are in the mirror,

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:05.300
it'll sense that and darken the window
as you can see in the slide there.

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:11.200
And so they have about $1.5 billion
per year sales for that product.

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:19.640
They also make the windows for
the Boeing 787 Dreamliner,

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:22.420
and here you can see one
of their photographs.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:27.080
And on the left you have
a completely clear window, and

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:31.380
then it's getting darker as
it goes over to the right.

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:38.880
I took some photographs myself when
I was in a Dreamliner recently.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:43.980
And I spent a good chunk
of the flight observing

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:49.490
how people were using the windows and
asking people how they liked it.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:51.980
And generally people, they do like it.

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:59.990
They like the fact that when the window is
darkened you can still see what's outside.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:04.220
You don't completely lose the view.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:09.420
The pilot loves it because they
can make all the windows go clear

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:13.560
before landing, which is a requirement.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:20.000
But you also see that because the windows
switches over a period of minutes.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:23.840
And a lot of people don't think it's
working when they push the button, and

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:26.960
so they keep pushing the button over and
over.

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:30.840
You also see that it's not
currently dark enough for

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:33.220
a lot of people if
they're trying to sleep.

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:40.450
And its very bluish, in fact in
the next slide you can see my hand

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:45.640
when the window is in the clear state and
then my hand when it's in the dark state.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:50.960
And you can see that
its a very blue window.

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:56.539
And you can imagine particularly
in homes and restaurants and

00:12:56.539 --> 00:13:02.580
office buildings people wouldn't
want to have that blue color.

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:06.800
And they wouldn't like the way
it makes other people appear.

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:14.750
So it's clearly important to
make the window switch faster,

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:20.280
to have them go darker, and
then to have a more neutral color.

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:23.820
I have read an article just in
the last few weeks that Gentex is

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:28.830
releasing a next generation of the window
that is supposed to be better.

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:31.755
But I haven't seen that, and

00:13:31.755 --> 00:13:36.788
I don't know what they did
to improve that window.

00:13:36.788 --> 00:13:41.031
So just to highlight some
of the needs here for

00:13:41.031 --> 00:13:47.245
a windows technology,
it needs to have uniform switching.

00:13:47.245 --> 00:13:51.635
And I should elaborate that a lot of the
technologies, they work well when they're

00:13:51.635 --> 00:13:56.375
small, but it's hard to make
a large window switch fast.

00:13:56.375 --> 00:14:01.320
And the reason is that when
the window's large it's

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:03.470
drawing a large amount of current.

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:08.560
And that when that current goes
through the transparent electrode,

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:11.750
there's a voltage drop,
just due to Ohm's Law.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:14.240
And then,
the switching depends on the voltage.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:19.850
And so the windows switch faster on
the edges where the voltage is high and

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:25.540
slower in the center, and
that leads to an effect call irising.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:31.680
So probably better
electrodes are needed or

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:35.540
windows that switch with a lower current.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:42.280
And then I've highlighted very high
contrast is needed, particularly

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:47.830
if privacy is needed,
say in a residential situation.

00:14:47.830 --> 00:14:53.030
You have to go below 0.1%
transmission to create

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:56.802
a state where people outside can't see in.

00:14:56.802 --> 00:14:59.990
And then of course the windows
need to be durable.

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:05.160
They should be stable in the resting
state, meaning that if you turn it dark,

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:12.440
it will stay dark, preferably without
having to continue to apply power.

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:16.590
And then it's very important that
the windows have very low haze,

00:15:16.590 --> 00:15:19.660
in other words,
that they not scatter light.

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:26.990
So with all that in mind, we wanted to
take a completely different approach.

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:32.910
We're not completely convinced that
the leading technologies out there

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:38.300
will ever have all of
the properties that are needed for

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:45.658
the most demanding applications like
windows in homes and commercial buildings.

00:15:45.658 --> 00:15:49.850
And we thought of a totally different way

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:54.810
that is to use metal
instead of metal oxides.

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:59.850
It only takes about 20 to 30
nanometers of metal To completely

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:07.090
block light you don't need microns,
just 20 to 30 nanometers.

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:12.390
And also, metals are very durable and

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:17.110
the challenge in this application,
is that, for

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:23.100
most of the situations, the materials
are exposed to full sunlight and

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:29.670
a wide range of temperatures, metals
can handle that without any trouble.

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:33.605
And then,
in the approach I'm about to show you,

00:16:33.605 --> 00:16:37.449
we're going to have
a transparent electrode, and

00:16:37.449 --> 00:16:42.699
then we're going to have just a metal
grid for the other electrode and

00:16:42.699 --> 00:16:48.063
then we're just going to inject
an electrolyte liquid in between.

00:16:48.063 --> 00:16:50.273
And that can be done quickly and

00:16:50.273 --> 00:16:55.810
very inexpensively compared to sputtering
a couple of microns of material.

00:16:57.790 --> 00:17:00.739
So, yeah, this next slide
just shows what we're doing.

00:17:00.739 --> 00:17:03.978
We're going to plate metal when
we want the window to darken.

00:17:03.978 --> 00:17:08.850
And then, we're just going to strip it,
when we want it to go clear.

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:16.185
We're, really, just moving metal
from a transparent conducting oxide

00:17:16.185 --> 00:17:21.495
over to just a metal
grid counter electrode.

00:17:24.217 --> 00:17:27.048
The post doc in my group, Chris Barile,

00:17:27.048 --> 00:17:32.560
thought carefully about what metals
should be used and he created this table.

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:38.070
And down at the bottom
of the table are some

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:44.640
candidate materials that
would be inexpensive,

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:48.200
but these materials are hard to plate.

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:52.180
And it would be very difficult
to reversibly plate them,

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:55.090
because they're very prone to oxidation.

00:17:55.090 --> 00:18:00.200
And once they are oxidized
then it's hard to strip them.

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:03.261
If we go up to the top, things like gold,

00:18:03.261 --> 00:18:08.225
platinum, palladium,
they're very resistant to oxidizing.

00:18:08.225 --> 00:18:14.911
However, perhaps for the same reason,
they're very expensive metals.

00:18:14.911 --> 00:18:20.729
So in between or some attractive
candidates, silver, copper,

00:18:20.729 --> 00:18:25.747
bismuth, lead, and
we found that they work very well.

00:18:25.747 --> 00:18:30.420
And we started with copper,
because it's well known that

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:35.510
copper can be electroplated and there's
a lot of literature on how to do it.

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:37.489
But copper by itself is red,

00:18:37.489 --> 00:18:41.953
so we needed to put another metal
in there to get a neutral color.

00:18:41.953 --> 00:18:45.601
We started with lead because there's so
much known about

00:18:45.601 --> 00:18:51.270
the electrochemistry of lead,
since it's used in lead acid batteries.

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:53.760
And we made great windows.

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:55.990
But the early feedback we got,

00:18:55.990 --> 00:19:02.860
is that it was a non starter
to have lead in these windows.

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:06.750
So we've moved on to silver,

00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:11.350
and we're also able to make
nice windows with that and

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:15.520
that's what's reported in our
manuscript in the journal, Joule.

00:19:17.881 --> 00:19:22.676
So I'll tell you a little bit
about how we got this working.

00:19:22.676 --> 00:19:26.100
Our first attempts were
on bare indium tin oxide.

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:32.698
And we had some success, but it didn't
work as well as we would have liked.

00:19:32.698 --> 00:19:38.617
Here, you can see,
scanning electron microscope images,

00:19:38.617 --> 00:19:45.348
and you see that there are certain
particles of the metals plating and

00:19:45.348 --> 00:19:49.900
it's not plating uniformly everywhere.

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:56.511
And light is able to go
in between the particles.

00:19:56.511 --> 00:20:01.827
And even worse than that,
when we cycled it 1,000 times and looked

00:20:01.827 --> 00:20:08.430
at the morphology with SEM, we found
that the morphology was changing a lot.

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:13.380
And we're reasonably sure that that
happens, because we don't think

00:20:13.380 --> 00:20:18.030
we're able to completely strip
the metal off of the indium tin oxide.

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:24.900
And the residual metal
influences the future growth.

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:33.105
And, here, you can see,
when we were doing a cycling experiment,

00:20:33.105 --> 00:20:37.725
where we would plate for 60 seconds and
then strip for 60 seconds.

00:20:37.725 --> 00:20:43.925
The minimum transmission was
getting worse and worse over time.

00:20:46.015 --> 00:20:50.814
And then, Chris decided that
the problem was that only

00:20:50.814 --> 00:20:55.950
some sites from the ITO are good for
plating metal.

00:20:55.950 --> 00:21:02.621
And so, he attached platinum
nano particles to the surface,

00:21:02.621 --> 00:21:08.396
knowing that metal will plate
more easily on platinum

00:21:08.396 --> 00:21:13.803
than it will on ITO, and
that worked really well.

00:21:13.803 --> 00:21:18.346
We still don't have a continuous
thing film of metal,

00:21:18.346 --> 00:21:22.594
we're still getting nano
particles of metal, but

00:21:22.594 --> 00:21:28.940
you can see in these SCM images,
that there's a pretty good distribution.

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:37.030
All patches on the window have
a fairly similar morphology.

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:42.610
And we found that when we
cycled it 1,000 times,

00:21:42.610 --> 00:21:48.630
we didn't get significant
changes in the morphology.

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:54.450
We believe that in the strip cycle,
all of the metal comes off and

00:21:54.450 --> 00:22:00.760
we're then starting over again
with a clean platinum surface.

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:06.640
And with that,
when we cycled it back and forth,

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:11.290
we got consistent results
over 1,000 cycles, and

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:17.410
I'll show even more cycles
later on in this presentation.

00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:22.239
Here, we're cycling between 90% and
40% transmission.

00:22:22.239 --> 00:22:23.970
We could go deeper.

00:22:25.760 --> 00:22:30.970
We're using a less aggressive cycling and

00:22:30.970 --> 00:22:37.110
we're just doing a short switch there,
to do 1,000 cycles, relatively quickly.

00:22:38.380 --> 00:22:45.150
This next slide shows just how
low the transmission can go.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:49.980
What you see here,
that after a few minutes,

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:56.550
the transmission is well below 1%.

00:22:56.550 --> 00:23:00.230
And a lot of people ask what
the switch time is, and

00:23:00.230 --> 00:23:06.640
that just depends on how dark
someone wants the window to go.

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:11.508
In, just say, ten seconds,
it's already down

00:23:11.508 --> 00:23:16.377
around 30%, which is clearly darkened, but

00:23:16.377 --> 00:23:22.449
it takes a few minutes to plate
enough metal to go below 1%.

00:23:22.449 --> 00:23:27.727
And this slide does illustrate that
this technology has a very wide range,

00:23:27.727 --> 00:23:31.035
because we can just keep plating metal.

00:23:31.035 --> 00:23:35.345
We can make the windows go as
dark as anyone wants them to go.

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:42.290
This next slide shows the spectrum.

00:23:42.290 --> 00:23:47.350
You've already seen pictures so you can
see that these things do look black.

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:54.180
And here's just quantitative data showing
how the spectrum is going down with time.

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:59.040
It also shows that this just keeps going
into the infrared and I'm not showing it

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:06.080
here, but The attenuation goes
out well past 2,000 nanometers,

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:11.070
which is as far as we've measured it,
and of course that makes complete sense.

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:16.910
This is metal and metal is very effective

00:24:16.910 --> 00:24:21.730
at blocking light over
an extremely wide spectral range.

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:28.400
And then you can see the spectrum
just return to its normal

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:34.410
state if we reverse the voltage.

00:24:34.410 --> 00:24:40.110
On the right you see two
days worth of our data,

00:24:40.110 --> 00:24:44.800
so first the device is put in it's opaque
state and then the power is removed,

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:47.540
and then you can see the metal stays put.

00:24:47.540 --> 00:24:54.670
And so with no power consumption
the window stays in it's dark state, and

00:24:54.670 --> 00:25:00.660
then at the 24 hour mark
the metal is stripped and

00:25:00.660 --> 00:25:05.840
then again the power is taken away, and
it'll stay in its transparent state.

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:09.940
So this technology consumes
very little power,

00:25:09.940 --> 00:25:15.826
just uses a small amount of power
during the switch and that's it.

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:26.380
Here you can see all the steps we use to
make a window, we start with a piece of

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:32.040
glass that you can see in the first box.

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:37.470
And then we put a butyl rubber
edge seal around there.

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:39.190
It's probably not optimized yet, but

00:25:39.190 --> 00:25:44.050
it's something we just happen to have
in our lab for packaging solar cells.

00:25:44.050 --> 00:25:48.950
And then, we put copper tape around there,

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:51.730
and that's going to be
the counter electrode.

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:58.815
For a window this size, we don't need a
fine grid of copper throughout the window.

00:25:58.815 --> 00:26:06.820
And then, we're putting on some
more rubber edge seal over that,

00:26:06.820 --> 00:26:11.760
then we have a second piece of
glass that has NTO on it, and

00:26:11.760 --> 00:26:17.420
it has copper tape around the edges
to help spread the current.

00:26:17.420 --> 00:26:22.594
So we'll be bringing the current in
from around the edge of the device,

00:26:22.594 --> 00:26:28.690
and in step five here we've
sandwiched all of that together.

00:26:29.968 --> 00:26:36.630
And then we're putting it on
a hot plate ant 120 degrees c.

00:26:36.630 --> 00:26:41.320
We put some weight on top of that and
that presses the two

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:46.270
pieces of glass together and the rubber
is a little bit softened up, and

00:26:46.270 --> 00:26:50.260
we get a nice seal between
the two pieces of glass.

00:26:50.260 --> 00:26:52.820
And then, coming over into step nine,

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:57.850
we have the electrolyte solution,
there's some polymer in there,

00:26:57.850 --> 00:27:02.890
hydroxyl oxycellulose,
which is also used in hair gel.

00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:06.920
And it's got the metal ions in there, and

00:27:06.920 --> 00:27:10.550
with one syringe and
needle we inject that.

00:27:10.550 --> 00:27:15.930
And then there's another
needle to help remove air from

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:20.770
the other side, and
that gives us a complete window.

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:27.970
This slide takes a more detailed,
look at some the updates,

00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:32.945
you can see that transmission
in a clear in the dark state.

00:27:32.945 --> 00:27:39.800
And in the clear state it
is clear in the visible,

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:45.110
but the ITO is absorbing
out in the infrared.

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:53.465
And then we see that there's not a whole
lot of reflection of the device,

00:27:53.465 --> 00:27:58.032
may be 10 to 20%.

00:27:58.032 --> 00:28:06.065
Most of the impact of plating
metal is in the absorption.

00:28:06.065 --> 00:28:12.860
And we see here we're getting about 80%
absorption across most of the film.

00:28:12.860 --> 00:28:18.710
And I don't think we understand
that in full delay yet, but having

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:24.310
this nano structured metal is causing
the light to get very heavily scattered.

00:28:24.310 --> 00:28:29.210
And then we're probably exciting a lot
of plasmonic resonances in the nano

00:28:29.210 --> 00:28:33.469
particles that's leading
to strong absorption.

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:42.050
But yeah, there is some debate, some
think it'd be great to reflect the light.

00:28:42.050 --> 00:28:46.470
And that would keep the window cool,
and it would just reject the heat.

00:28:46.470 --> 00:28:51.410
But a lot of people we've talked to
actually prefer the absorption and

00:28:51.410 --> 00:28:55.440
the black color, and point out that you

00:28:55.440 --> 00:29:00.760
really don't want your window
to look like a mirror.

00:29:00.760 --> 00:29:04.660
And there're even laws in Singapore, for

00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:10.640
example saying that the windows
cannot reflect more than 30%,

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:15.520
because you can just send
light over to other building.

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:19.750
And also if there's a little
bit of curvature in the window,

00:29:19.750 --> 00:29:26.510
you can have a light focusing effect and
certain spots can get very hot.

00:29:26.510 --> 00:29:30.190
So for a lot of the applications,
it's probably very good

00:29:30.190 --> 00:29:34.360
that these films are absorptive and black.

00:29:37.528 --> 00:29:44.800
I think it's also worth explaining that
these films do not obey Beer's Law.

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:50.720
I'll admit when we designed the project,
that was the plan, but

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:55.610
the transmission does not drop
exponentially with film thickness.

00:29:55.610 --> 00:29:59.240
And that's because we don't
have metal everywhere and

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:03.343
so more like gets through than
you would expect from Beer's Law.

00:30:03.343 --> 00:30:10.930
Because light is going in
between the particles,

00:30:10.930 --> 00:30:15.890
and there's some I think good and
bad news associated with that.

00:30:15.890 --> 00:30:20.950
Probably the good side of it is
that our transmission versus

00:30:20.950 --> 00:30:26.480
deposition time curve is not quite
as steep as the Beer's Law curve.

00:30:26.480 --> 00:30:32.740
And that means that we can have just
slight variations in film thickness,

00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:37.700
and not have a huge you know
variation in transmission.

00:30:37.700 --> 00:30:43.900
But I will say we're going to at least
try to have the metal plate everywhere.

00:30:43.900 --> 00:30:49.540
Because if it does and we get to
the Beer's Law curve, then we will

00:30:49.540 --> 00:30:55.810
get low transmissions with less metal,
and that will mean with less current.

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:59.480
And that'll mean that we can
switch the window faster

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:10.130
And here's data for
a window with a cooper,

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:15.670
silver, gel electrolyte,
we cycle that 5,500 times.

00:31:15.670 --> 00:31:22.390
And then at that point, decided that we
needed to use to potentious stats for

00:31:22.390 --> 00:31:28.390
other experiments, but
it was very exciting that we didn't see

00:31:28.390 --> 00:31:33.010
degradation due to cycling
after that many cycles.

00:31:35.830 --> 00:31:39.390
So just to review some of
the highlights here, I think

00:31:40.700 --> 00:31:45.310
key advantage that we have with
reversible metal electrodeposition

00:31:45.310 --> 00:31:50.780
compared to electrochromics
based on transition metal oxide.

00:31:50.780 --> 00:31:56.110
Is the gray color in
the partially tinting state,

00:31:56.110 --> 00:32:01.290
and the black color when
it's highly opaque.

00:32:01.290 --> 00:32:04.140
Right now I think our switching
speeds are comparable.

00:32:05.800 --> 00:32:09.920
People who read our paper are excited
about the fast switch but

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:16.120
to be fair to the competition,
if we made meter by meter windows,

00:32:16.120 --> 00:32:21.060
we would have to run them slower
as well to prevent the irising.

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:27.220
However, I'm optimistic that we
can improve the metal morphology

00:32:27.220 --> 00:32:32.150
and get to a situation
where we do switch two to

00:32:32.150 --> 00:32:37.870
five times faster for
a window of the same size.

00:32:37.870 --> 00:32:43.250
I think it would be premature for
me to put out a number on costs,

00:32:43.250 --> 00:32:47.210
particularly as a professor
who's never run a factory.

00:32:47.210 --> 00:32:52.840
But if I just compare this
directly to the electrochromics,

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:56.430
I think it's very promising
that it can be cheaper,

00:32:56.430 --> 00:33:02.350
will have less material that is
sputtered in a vacuum chamber.

00:33:02.350 --> 00:33:07.130
And so I think there's good
potential to reduce cost.

00:33:07.130 --> 00:33:12.600
And then a last advantage is the very
high range of transmission and

00:33:12.600 --> 00:33:15.710
that we can go dark
enough to have privacy.

00:33:17.629 --> 00:33:21.270
So when we look forward to our challenges.

00:33:21.270 --> 00:33:29.020
It would be really wonderful if
a large window could switch rapidly.

00:33:29.020 --> 00:33:34.590
We heard, we've been told that it's
not necessarily a requirement for

00:33:34.590 --> 00:33:40.970
architectural applications that certainly
a smart control system knows what the sun

00:33:40.970 --> 00:33:47.630
is going to do and can start darkening
the window before the glare gets too bad.

00:33:47.630 --> 00:33:53.270
But we hear other people say, especially
those who want to put this in automobile

00:33:53.270 --> 00:33:58.930
car roofs, that you really want to fast
switch when people press a button.

00:33:58.930 --> 00:34:02.610
They want to see something
happen right away otherwise they

00:34:02.610 --> 00:34:04.180
might think it's broken.

00:34:05.810 --> 00:34:11.370
And so,
if the improvement here it might be

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:16.778
in having the metal more effectively block
light so that we can use less of it.

00:34:16.778 --> 00:34:23.040
But it's probably more in making a better
electrode, and we've got some ideas for

00:34:24.390 --> 00:34:29.195
incorporating metal grid
lines into the electrodes so

00:34:29.195 --> 00:34:33.775
that we can pass more current
without a voltage drop.

00:34:33.775 --> 00:34:39.945
And then of course long term
stability is going to be crucial

00:34:39.945 --> 00:34:44.425
and we need to make sure not
only we can cycle it but

00:34:44.425 --> 00:34:47.220
it has to handle ultra violet light.

00:34:47.220 --> 00:34:52.090
We can't have the polymer gel
break down in the UV light.

00:34:52.090 --> 00:34:58.200
We have to make sure that it can handle
an elevated temperature for a long time.

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:03.594
We are seeing some issues with
the MDM10 oxide being etched,

00:35:03.594 --> 00:35:08.800
and we're moving towards more
stable electrodes for that.

00:35:10.882 --> 00:35:16.420
And finally I'd like to thank
a really outstanding team.

00:35:16.420 --> 00:35:22.700
Chris Barile, was the post doc who
developed all of the chemistry for

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:28.406
this project and he's now a professor
at the University of Nevada, Reno.

00:35:28.406 --> 00:35:35.220
And Dan has interacted with a lot of

00:35:35.220 --> 00:35:40.860
the companies as part of a class
he took on entrepreneurship and

00:35:40.860 --> 00:35:45.905
learned a lot about what the needs are for
dynamic windows,

00:35:45.905 --> 00:35:50.155
and did a lot of the work in
the optics and building the package.

00:35:50.155 --> 00:35:57.505
And Tyler, and Michael, and
Teresa are developing some

00:35:57.505 --> 00:36:02.505
even better chemistries that
I haven't shown you yet, but

00:36:02.505 --> 00:36:06.680
you will see some even better
windows down the future.

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:11.290
And then finally I really want to thank
the Stanford Precourt Institute for

00:36:11.290 --> 00:36:15.350
Energy for giving us a seed
grant to get this going and

00:36:15.350 --> 00:36:19.350
this was a completely new area for
our group and

00:36:19.350 --> 00:36:24.160
it was great to have someone give us
this chance to try something new.

00:36:25.290 --> 00:36:29.990
And I thank you all for your attention and
I hope you enjoyed this presentation.

00:36:32.230 --> 00:36:35.270
&gt;&gt; Thank you again for joining us for
the presentation today.

